{"acceptedResponses":["1"],"answer":"1","area":"Geometry","bank":"cn-math","batch":"cnm_264","choiceOrder":[],"choices":{},"choicesType":"text","domain":["domain:geometry"],"editorialNote":"","figureSources":{},"grade":9,"id":"cnm_264_q1","knowledgeGraph":{"assessedIds":["cnm_264"],"contentHash":"9dc328a440c654456d03e15ec046a91bf5492d8e437e6787b0b3f285630457c1","flags":[],"reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"area-semiperimeter","label":"Area and semiperimeter","methodDirectNodeIds":{"area-semiperimeter":["math:triangle-area-radius-relations","cnm_p148"]},"nodeIds":["math:triangle-area-radius-relations","cnm_p148"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"tangent-segments","label":"Equal tangent segments","methodDirectNodeIds":{"tangent-segments":["cnm_263","cnm_262","cnm_191"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_263","cnm_262","cnm_191"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_174"],"id":"right-triangle-inradius-formula","label":"Right-triangle inradius formula","methodDirectNodeIds":{"right-triangle-inradius-formula":["cnm_264"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_264","cnm_174"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_174","cnm_p148"],"id":"common-height-area-decomposition","label":"Use the inradius as a common height","methodDirectNodeIds":{"common-height-area-decomposition":["cnm_264","math:triangle-area-radius-relations","cnm_p150"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_264","math:triangle-area-radius-relations","cnm_p150","cnm_174","cnm_p148"]}],"status":"mapped","supportIds":["cnm_174"],"version":"1.0.0"},"level":"Middle school","number":1,"question":"What is the inradius of a right triangle with legs 3 and 4?","questionType":"text","rejectedAnswers":[],"responseType":"numeric","schemaVersion":"3.0","skill":[],"solution":"The hypotenuse is 5. The inradius of a right triangle is \\(r=(a+b-c)/2=(3+4-5)/2=1\\).","solutionAvailability":"present","solutionMethods":[{"directNodeIds":["cnm_264"],"explanation":"The hypotenuse is 5. The inradius of a right triangle is \\(r=(a+b-c)/2=(3+4-5)/2=1\\).","id":"right-triangle-inradius-formula","label":"Right-triangle inradius formula","legacyRouteId":"right-triangle-inradius-formula","provenance":{"contribution":"Existing attributed explanation retained; project contribution is review and annotations.","evidence":[{"label":"Accepted primary worked-solution review","path":"../database/knowledge-graph/evidence/cn-solutions-2026-11-06/cnm_264_q1.json","sha256":"13998faa3e91c72f34a1bd5bce670db4b7a6cdb0e724570208bc5f610a6b7d63"}],"license":"CC-BY-NC-4.0","licenseURL":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/","reviewStatus":"Current explanation retained and independently AI-reviewed; not teacher reviewed","source":"cn-math-taxonomy (AI-generated; not teacher reviewed)","sourceRef":"cn-math/cnm_264_q1#right-triangle-inradius-formula","sourceRevision":"62b341d4b66b175413d6e3f697e37639fd96aa2d","sourceURL":"https://github.com/xiaoyang111222/cn-math-taxonomy"}},{"directNodeIds":["cnm_264","math:triangle-area-radius-relations","cnm_p150"],"explanation":"Joining the incenter to the three vertices divides the triangle into three smaller triangles. Their heights to the original sides all equal the inradius \\(r\\), which lets us express the whole area in terms of \\(r\\). The hypotenuse is \\(\\sqrt{3^2+4^2}=5\\), so\n\\[\n\\frac{3\\times4}{2}=\\frac{3r}{2}+\\frac{4r}{2}+\\frac{5r}{2}=6r.\n\\]\nThus \\(6=6r\\), and the inradius is \\(r=1\\).","id":"common-height-area-decomposition","label":"Use the inradius as a common height","legacyRouteId":"common-height-area-decomposition","provenance":{"contribution":"New project-authored alternative alongside the retained attributed explanation.","evidence":[{"label":"Accepted primary worked-solution review","path":"../database/knowledge-graph/evidence/cn-solutions-2026-11-06/cnm_264_q1.json","sha256":"13998faa3e91c72f34a1bd5bce670db4b7a6cdb0e724570208bc5f610a6b7d63"}],"license":"CC-BY-NC-4.0","licenseURL":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/","reviewStatus":"AI-authored and independently AI-reviewed;